---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import {symbolSections as sections,reusedSymbols as reused,symbolRows,symbolId,symbolLookup} from '../lib/symbols.mjs';
const rowFor=id=>symbolRows.find(row=>symbolId(row)===id);
---
<Layout title="Physics Symbols: List of Symbols, Meanings and Units" description="List of physics symbols with their meanings and SI units: F for force, d for distance, v for velocity, μ for friction, λ for wavelength and Ω for the ohm." section="Formulas" schemaType="Article"><article class="article"><Breadcrumbs items={[['Home','/'],['Formula sheet','/formulas/'],['Physics symbols']]}/><div class="eyebrow">Small letters. Big meanings.</div><h1>Physics symbols: list with meanings and units</h1><p class="lesson-lead">Physics formulas are written in symbols: F for force, d for distance, m for mass, λ for wavelength, μ for the coefficient of friction. This list gives {symbolRows.length} symbols used in introductory physics, each with its meaning, SI unit, and a formula where you will meet it. Start with the A–Z table to find the symbol for a quantity, or go to a topic. Some letters mean different things in different topics, so always check the context.</p><nav class="related" aria-label="Symbol topics"><a href="#by-quantity">Symbols A–Z by quantity</a>{sections.map(([id,name])=><a href={`#${id}`}>{name}</a>)}<a href="#same-letter">Same letter, different meaning</a></nav><section class="symbol-section" id="by-quantity"><h2>Find the symbol for a quantity (A–Z)</h2><p>Look up the quantity to see its usual symbol and SI unit. Each name links to the full row with a formula.</p><div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Symbol</th><th scope="col">SI unit</th></tr></thead><tbody>{symbolLookup.map(([quantity,symbol,id])=><tr><td><a class="text-link" href={`#${id}`}>{quantity}</a></td><td><code>{symbol}</code></td><td>{rowFor(id)[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>{sections.map(([id,name,rows],i)=><><section class="symbol-section" id={id}><h2>{name} symbols</h2><div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Symbol</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">SI unit</th><th scope="col">Example formula</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(row=>{const [symbol,meaning,unit,formula,slug]=row;return <tr id={symbolId(row)}><td><code>{symbol}</code></td><td>{meaning}</td><td>{unit}</td><td>{slug?<a class="text-link" href={`/experiments/${slug}/`}>{formula}</a>:formula}</td></tr>;})}</tbody></table></div></section>{i===0&&<AdSlot type="rectangle"/>}{i===3&&<AdSlot type="rectangle"/>}</>)}<section class="symbol-section" id="same-letter"><h2>Same letter, different meaning</h2><p>A handful of letters do double duty. The units usually tell you which quantity is meant.</p><div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Possible meanings</th></tr></thead><tbody>{reused.map(([symbol,meaning])=><tr><td><code>{symbol}</code></td><td>{meaning}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section><h2>Subscripts and Greek letters</h2><p>A subscript zero, as in v₀ or x₀, marks the starting value. Δ (delta) means “change in”, so Δx is final position minus initial position. Σ (sigma) means “sum of”, so ΣF is the net force. Greek letters often name angles (θ), densities (ρ), friction coefficients (μ), wavelengths (λ), and angular frequencies (ω).</p><div class="related"><a href="/formulas/">Physics formulas sheet ↗</a><a href="/units/">Physics units & constants ↗</a><a href="/experiments/">Physics simulations ↗</a></div></article></Layout>
